<!-- Accessibility: every image carries a <shortdescription> (alt text)    -->
<!-- and a long <description> for screen readers.                          -->

<section xml:id="sec-arc-length">
  <title>Arc Length of a Curve</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When an object moves along a curve, we often want to know the total
      distance it travels. In this section we build the arc-length integral,
      first in the plane and then in space, and we apply it to two motions:
      a point sweeping out a quarter circle and a paraglider spiralling to
      the ground. The key idea throughout is simple:
      <em>distance travelled is speed accumulated over time</em>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arc-length-2d">
    <title>Arc Length in 2D</title>

    <p>
      <term>Goal.</term> Compute the arc length <m>S</m> of a curve using
      its parametrized form. Imagine that at time <m>t=a</m> an object
      starts moving from an initial point <m>A</m> and at time <m>t=b</m>
      it reaches the final point <m>B</m>. During this period the object
      traces out a smooth curve <m>C</m> in the plane. Throughout this
      section we illustrate the construction on the closed curve of
      <xref ref="fig-arc-length-curve"/>, a circle traversed once
      counterclockwise, so that the object returns to where it began and
      <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are the same point. How can we calculate the
      distance the object has travelled?
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-arc-length-curve">
      <caption>
        A smooth curve <m>C</m> traced by a moving object, from the starting
        point <m>A</m> at time <m>t=a</m> to the ending point <m>B</m> at
        time <m>t=b</m>. Here <m>C</m> is closed, so the object returns to
        its starting point and <m>A=B</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-arc-length-curve" width="65%">
        <shortdescription>
          A circle labelled C on a pair of coordinate axes, with an arrow
          giving the direction of travel and the start and end point marked.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A circle of radius three, labelled C, is drawn on a pair of
            coordinate axes with equally scaled x and y directions. An
            arrowhead on the curve shows that the object travels
            counterclockwise. A single red dot on the positive x
            axis, where the circle crosses it, marks both the starting point
            A, reached at time t equals a, and the ending point B, reached at
            time t equals b, since the object returns to where it began after
            going once around.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure label="arc-length-curve" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <!-- Shares its dimensions, margins and bbox with
               fig-arc-length-chords so the two figures show the same curve
               at the same scale. aspect-ratio="1" keeps the x and y
               directions equally scaled, so the circle is round. -->
          <diagram dimensions="(469,417)" margins="5">
            <definition>R = 3</definition>
            <definition>C(t) = (R*cos(t), R*sin(t))</definition>
            <definition>Start = C(0)</definition>

            <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-4.3,5.9,5.0)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <axes at="ax" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>

              <group at="curve-C">
                <parametric-curve function="C" domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="black"
                                  thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="pi/2"/>
                <label anchor="C(2.6)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)">
                  <m>C</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="start-end">
                <point p="Start" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="(R,0.5)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)" color="red"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>A=B</m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="(R,-0.5)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)" color="red"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=a,\ t=b</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="A smooth closed curve C, drawn as a circle of radius three, traced by a moving object that starts at the point A at time t equals a and returns to it as the point B at time t equals b">
                <annotation ref="ax" text="The coordinate axes, equally scaled"/>
                <annotation ref="curve-C"
                            text="The curve C, traversed counterclockwise in the direction of increasing time"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="start-end"
                            text="The point where the journey begins and ends: the starting point A at time t equals a and the ending point B at time t equals b"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The strategy is to replace the curve by a chain of straight segments
      whose lengths we already know how to compute. Partition the time
      interval into <m>n</m> pieces,
      <m>a=t_0\lt t_1\lt\cdots\lt t_n=b</m>, mark the corresponding points
      on the curve, and join consecutive ones by chords, as in
      <xref ref="fig-arc-length-chords"/>. Over the <m>k</m>-th step the
      object is displaced <m>\Delta x_k</m> horizontally and
      <m>\Delta y_k</m> vertically, so by the Pythagorean theorem the
      <m>k</m>-th chord length <m>\Delta s_k</m> satisfies

    <men xml:id="eq-chord-pythagoras">
      \left(\Delta s_k\right)^{2}
        = \left(\Delta x_k\right)^{2} + \left(\Delta y_k\right)^{2}.
    </men>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-arc-length-chords">
      <caption>
        Approximating the length of a curve <m>C</m> by an inscribed
        polygon with <m>n</m> sides. The <m>k</m>-th chord joins the point
        reached at time <m>t_{k-1}</m> to the point reached at time
        <m>t_k</m>; its length <m>\Delta s_k</m> is the hypotenuse of a
        right triangle with legs <m>\Delta x_k</m> and <m>\Delta y_k</m>.
        The corresponding time step is
        <m>\Delta t_k=t_k-t_{k-1}</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-arc-length-chords" width="65%">
        <shortdescription>
          A closed curve with an inscribed dashed polygon; one chord is
          highlighted together with its horizontal and vertical legs,
          labelled delta x sub k and delta y sub k.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A circle of radius three, labelled C, is drawn on a pair of
            coordinate axes. Inside it a dashed green polygon with nine
            equal sides is inscribed, its vertices marked by small green
            dots lying on the curve; these are the points reached at the
            partition times. In the upper right of the circle one side of
            the polygon is drawn as a thick solid green chord and labelled
            delta s sub k. Its two endpoints are marked in red
            and labelled t sub k minus one, the lower right endpoint, and
            t sub k, the upper left endpoint. A dashed orange horizontal
            segment labelled delta x sub k runs from beneath t sub k across
            to t sub k minus one, and a dotted blue vertical segment
            labelled delta y sub k runs from the right end of that
            horizontal segment up to t sub k, so that the chord is the
            hypotenuse of a right triangle. A small square marks the right
            angle where the two legs meet. Above the figure a red note
            reads delta t sub k equals t sub k minus t sub k minus one.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure label="arc-length-chords" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <!-- Shares its dimensions, margins and bbox with
               fig-arc-length-curve so the two figures show the same curve
               at the same scale. aspect-ratio="1" keeps the x and y
               directions equally scaled, so the circle is round. -->
          <diagram dimensions="(469,417)" margins="5">
            <definition>R = 3</definition>
            <definition>N = 9</definition>
            <definition>V(k) = (R*cos(2*pi*k/N), R*sin(2*pi*k/N))</definition>
            <definition>Pprev = V(1)</definition>
            <definition>Pk = V(2)</definition>
            <definition>Q = (Pk[0], Pprev[1])</definition>
            <definition>Mx = ((Pprev[0]+Pk[0])/2, Pprev[1])</definition>
            <definition>My = (Pk[0], (Pprev[1]+Pk[1])/2)</definition>
            <definition>Ms = ((Pprev[0]+Pk[0])/2, (Pprev[1]+Pk[1])/2)</definition>
            <definition>sq = 0.25</definition>

            <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-4.3,5.9,5.0)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <axes at="ax" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>

              <group at="curve-C">
                <circle center="(0,0)" radius="R" stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
                <label anchor="(R*cos(2.6), R*sin(2.6))" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)">
                  <m>C</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="inscribed-polygon">
                <polygon parameter="k=0..8" points="V(k)" closed="yes"
                         stroke="#009E73" thickness="2" dash="7 5"/>
                <repeat parameter="k=0..8">
                  <point p="V(k)" size="3.5" stroke="#009E73" fill="#009E73"/>
                </repeat>
              </group>

              <group at="chord">
                <line endpoints="(Pprev, Pk)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="4"/>
                <label anchor="Ms" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)" color="#009E73">
                  <m>\Delta s_k</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="legs">
                <line endpoints="(Q, Pprev)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
                <line endpoints="(Q, Pk)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2" dash="2 4"/>
                <polygon points="[(Q[0]+sq, Q[1]), (Q[0]+sq, Q[1]+sq), (Q[0], Q[1]+sq)]"
                         closed="no" stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
                <label anchor="Mx" alignment="s" offset="(0,-6)" color="#D55E00"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>\Delta x_k</m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="My" alignment="w" offset="(-16,0)" color="#0072B2"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>\Delta y_k</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="nodes">
                <point p="Pprev" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <point p="Pk" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="Pprev" alignment="e" offset="(8,-2)" color="red"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t_{k-1}</m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="Pk" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,6)" color="red"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t_k</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <label at="dt-note" anchor="(0.9,4.5)" alignment="e" color="red">
                <m>\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}</m>
              </label>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="A curve C approximated by an inscribed polygon with nine sides. One side, the k-th chord, is highlighted together with its horizontal leg delta x sub k and its vertical leg delta y sub k">
                <annotation ref="ax" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="curve-C"
                            text="The curve C whose length we want to measure, drawn here as a circle of radius three"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="inscribed-polygon"
                            text="The inscribed polygon whose nine vertices are the points reached at the partition times t sub 0 through t sub n"/>
                <annotation ref="chord"
                            text="The k-th chord, of length delta s sub k, joining the point at time t sub k minus one to the point at time t sub k"/>
                <annotation ref="legs"
                            text="The legs of the right triangle under the k-th chord: a horizontal leg of length delta x sub k and a vertical leg of length delta y sub k, meeting at a right angle"/>
                <annotation ref="nodes"
                            text="The endpoints of the k-th chord, reached at times t sub k minus one and t sub k"/>
                <annotation ref="dt-note"
                            text="A note recording the k-th time step, delta t sub k equals t sub k minus t sub k minus one"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      We approximate the arc length <m>S</m> by summing <m>\Delta s_k</m>
      as <m>k</m> changes from <m>1</m> to the number of divisions
      <m>n</m>:

    <me>
      S \approx \sum_{k=1}^{n}\Delta s_k
        = \sum_{k=1}^{n}
          \sqrt{\left(\Delta x_k\right)^{2}+\left(\Delta y_k\right)^{2}}.
    </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The inscribed polygon hugs the curve more and more closely as the
      chords get shorter, so we recover the exact length in the limit:

    <md>
      <mrow>
        S \amp = \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\Delta s_k
             = \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}
               \sqrt{\left(\Delta x_k\right)^{2}+\left(\Delta y_k\right)^{2}}
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        S \amp = \int_C ds
             = \int_C \sqrt{\left(dx\right)^{2}+\left(dy\right)^{2}}.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This is a fine formula, but it is not yet something we can evaluate:
      there is no variable of integration. To bring the parameter <m>t</m>
      into the picture, we divide and multiply by
      <m>\Delta t_k=t_k-t_{k-1}</m>:

    <me>
      S \approx \sum_{k=1}^{n}\Delta s_k
        = \sum_{k=1}^{n}
          \sqrt{\left(\frac{\Delta x_k}{\Delta t_k}\right)^{2}
              + \left(\frac{\Delta y_k}{\Delta t_k}\right)^{2}}\;
          \Delta t_k.
    </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      We then take <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty}</m> to find the exact arc
      length. Each difference quotient turns into a derivative, and the
      sum turns into an integral over time:

    <md>
      <mrow>
        S \amp = \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}
               \sqrt{\left(\frac{\Delta x_k}{\Delta t_k}\right)^{2}
                   + \left(\frac{\Delta y_k}{\Delta t_k}\right)^{2}}\;
               \Delta t_k
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \amp = \int_{t=a}^{t=b}
               \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^{2}
                   + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^{2}}\,dt
             = \int_{t=a}^{t=b}
               \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt,
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      where in the last step we recognized the square root as the length of
      the velocity vector, that is, as the object's speed.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-arc-length-2d-results">
      <title>Results</title>
      <p>
        Let a plane curve <m>C</m> be parametrized by
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle x(t),\,y(t)\right\rangle</m> for
        <m>a\le t\le b</m>. Then

      <men xml:id="eq-arc-length-2d">
        S = \int_C ds
          = \int_{t=a}^{t=b}\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt,
      </men>
      </p>
      <p>
        where

      <men xml:id="eq-line-element-2d">
        \mathbf{v}(t)=\frac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt}
          =\left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\ \frac{dy}{dt}\right\rangle,
        \qquad
        ds = \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt.
      </men>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      In words: <em>arc length is the integral of speed over time</em>. This
      is the natural generalization of the familiar rule
      <q>distance equals speed times time</q> to motion whose speed and
      direction change from moment to moment.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE 1: quarter circle. Statement only; solution is a separate  -->
  <!-- <solution> environment placed after the <statement>.               -->
  <example xml:id="ex-quarter-circle">
    <title>Length of a quarter circle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the arc-length formula <xref ref="eq-arc-length-2d"/>,
        calculate the length of a
        quarter circle of radius one. (We already know the answer: a full
        unit circle has circumference <m>2\pi</m>, so a quarter of it has
        length <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>. The point of this example is to see
        the formula reproduce it.)
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We can parametrize the quarter circle as
        <me>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos t,\ \sin t\right\rangle,
            \qquad 0\le t\le\frac{\pi}{2}.</me>
        Differentiating, the velocity vector and its length are

      <md>
        <mrow>\mathbf{v}(t) \amp
              = \left\langle-\sin t,\ \cos t\right\rangle,</mrow>
        <mrow>\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert \amp
              = \sqrt{\sin^2 t+\cos^2 t}=1.</mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The speed is constant and equal to <m>1</m>, so the arc length is
        simply the elapsed time:

      <me>
        S = \int_C ds
          = \int_0^{\pi/2}\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt
          = \int_0^{\pi/2} 1\,dt = \frac{\pi}{2},
      </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        as expected. Notice a useful by-product: because the speed is
        <m>1</m>, the arc length swept out up to time <m>t</m> is
        <m>s(t)=t</m> exactly. The motion is shown in
        <xref ref="fig-quarter-circle-video"/>, the position and velocity
        vectors in <xref ref="fig-quarter-circle-prefigure"/>, and a snapshot
        of the partially swept arc in <xref ref="fig-quarter-circle-tikz"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-video">
        <caption>
          The point
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos t,\ \sin t\right\rangle</m>
          sweeps out the quarter circle as <m>t</m> increases from <m>0</m>
          to <m>\pi/2</m>; the accumulated arc length <m>s(t)=t</m> reaches
          <m>\pi/2\approx 1.5708</m>.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-quarter-circle" width="80%"
               source="videos/quarter_circle_arclength_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-prefigure">
        <caption>
          The quarter circle
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle\cos t,\ \sin t\right\rangle</m> with
          the position vector (orange) and the unit velocity vector
          <m>\mathbf{v}(t)</m> (green), which is tangent to the curve.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-quarter-circle-arclength" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A quarter of the unit circle from the point one comma zero to
              the point zero comma one on a coordinate grid, with an orange
              position vector from the origin to a point on the arc and a
              green unit velocity vector tangent to the arc at that point.
            </p>
          </description>
          <shortdescription>
          A quarter of the unit circle with position and velocity vectors drawn.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure label="quarter-circle-arclength" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
              <definition>r(t)=(cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
              <definition>t0=0.9</definition>
              <definition>P=r(t0)</definition>
              <definition>vel=(-sin(t0), cos(t0))</definition>

              <coordinates bbox="[-0.35,-0.35,1.5,1.5]">
                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" spacing="(0.5,0.5)"/>

                <group at="arc">
                  <parametric-curve function="r" domain="(0,pi/2)" stroke="blue"
                                    thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="1.35"/>
                  <label anchor="r(0.5)" alignment="ne" offset="(12,10)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>

                <group at="endpoints">
                  <point p="(1,0)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                  <label anchor="(1,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,4)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(1,0)</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="(0,1)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                  <label anchor="(0,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,4)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(0,1)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>

                <group at="radius">
                  <vector v="P" tail="(0,0)" stroke="orange"/>
                  <point p="P" size="3.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                </group>

                <group at="velocity">
                  <vector v="vel" tail="P" stroke="green"/>
                  <label anchor="P+0.5*vel" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>\mathbf{v}(t)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
              </coordinates>

              <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure"
                            text="A quarter of the unit circle parametrized by r of t equals cosine t, sine t, for t between 0 and pi over 2, showing the position and unit velocity vectors">
                  <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                  <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                  <annotation ref="arc"
                              text="The quarter circle arc from the point 1 comma 0 to the point 0 comma 1"
                              sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="endpoints" text="The endpoints 1 comma 0 and 0 comma 1"/>
                  <annotation ref="radius" text="The position vector r of t from the origin to a point on the arc"/>
                  <annotation ref="velocity"
                              text="The velocity vector v of t, tangent to the arc, which has length one"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-tikz">
        <caption>
          Snapshot at <m>t=\pi/4</m>: the arc already swept out (blue) has
          length <m>s(t)=t=\pi/4</m>, the part not yet traced is gray, and the
          unit velocity vector <m>\mathbf{v}(t)</m> is tangent to the circle
          at the moving point.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-quarter-circle-tikz" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            A point partway along the unit quarter circle, with its position
            vector, unit velocity vector, and the arc swept so far.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              The first quadrant with a light coordinate grid. A quarter
              circle of radius one runs from the point (1,0) up to the point
              (0,1). The portion already travelled, from (1,0)
              counterclockwise to the moving point at t equals pi over four, is a
              thick blue arc ending in an arrowhead; the portion not yet
              travelled is gray. An orange position vector labelled r of t
              reaches from the origin to the moving point, drawn in red. A
              green vector labelled v of t, of length one, starts at the
              moving point, tangent to the circle and pointing in the
              direction of motion. A blue label reads s of t equals t, the
              arc length swept so far.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3.2, &gt;={Stealth[length=6pt]}]
              % light grid
              \draw[gray!25, very thin] (-0.3,-0.3) grid[step=0.5] (1.4,1.4);
              % axes
              \draw[-&gt;] (-0.35,0) -- (1.55,0) node[right] {\(x\)};
              \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.35) -- (0,1.55) node[above] {\(y\)};
              % remaining part of the quarter circle (still to be traced)
              % (pi/4 rad = 45 degrees)
              \draw[gray!60, thick] (45:1) arc[start angle=45, end angle=90, radius=1];
              % arc already swept out, from t=0 to t=pi/4
              \draw[blue, very thick, -&gt;] (1,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=45, radius=1];
              % endpoints
              \fill (1,0) circle (0.55pt) node[below right=-1pt] {\((1,0)\)};
              \fill (0,1) circle (0.55pt) node[above right=-1pt] {\((0,1)\)};
              % position vector r(t)
              \draw[-&gt;, orange, thick] (0,0) -- (0.7071,0.7071)
                  node[pos=0.55, above left=-2pt] {\(\mathbf r(t)\)};
              % moving point
              \fill[red] (0.7071,0.7071) circle (0.9pt);
              % unit velocity vector v(t), components -sin t and cos t
              \draw[-&gt;, green!55!black, thick] (0.7071,0.7071) -- ++(-0.7071,0.7071)
                  node[pos=0.6, above right=-2pt] {\(\mathbf v(t)\)};
              % arc-length readout
              \node[blue] at (1.05,1.25) {\(s(t)=t\)};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arc-length-3d">
    <title>Arc Length in 3D</title>

    <p>
      The formulas <xref ref="eq-arc-length-2d"/> and
      <xref ref="eq-line-element-2d"/> generalize to motion in space with
      no new ideas:
      the <m>k</m>-th chord of <xref ref="eq-chord-pythagoras"/> simply
      picks up a third leg, so
      <m>\left(\Delta s_k\right)^{2}
         = \left(\Delta x_k\right)^{2}+\left(\Delta y_k\right)^{2}
          +\left(\Delta z_k\right)^{2}</m>
      and in the limit <m>ds=\sqrt{(dx)^2+(dy)^2+(dz)^2}</m>. For a space
      curve
      <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle x(t),\,y(t),\,z(t)\right\rangle</m> we
      therefore have

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-arc-length-3d" number="yes">
        S \amp
          = \int_C ds
          = \int_{t=a}^{t=b} \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^{2}
                         + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^{2}
                         + \left(\frac{dz}{dt}\right)^{2}}\,dt
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \amp = \int_{t=a}^{t=b}
               \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{v}(t) \amp
          = \frac{d\mathbf{r}(t)}{dt}
          = \left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\ \frac{dy}{dt},\
                         \frac{dz}{dt}\right\rangle,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert \amp
          = \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^{2}
                + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^{2}
                + \left(\frac{dz}{dt}\right)^{2}}.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      As in the plane, arc length is still the integral of speed over time.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE 2: paraglider. Statement (parts A,B,C); solution separate. -->
  <example xml:id="ex-paraglider">
    <title>Distance travelled by a paraglider</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The position of a paraglider at time <m>t</m> is
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle t\cos t,\ t\sin t,\
            9\sqrt{8}-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}\,t^{3/2}\right\rangle.
        </me>
        Assuming that the paraglider begins at time <m>t=0</m>:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            How long does it take to reach the ground, that is,
            <m>z=0</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate the velocity vector at time <m>t</m>, and also
            calculate its length.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Calculate the distance travelled by the paraglider.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The paraglider reaches the ground when the
        <m>z</m>-coordinate vanishes:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            9\sqrt{8}-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}\,t^{3/2} \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \Longrightarrow\quad t^{3/2} \amp = 27
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \Longrightarrow\quad t \amp = 27^{2/3}=9.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        So it takes <m>9</m> units of time to land.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> We differentiate each component. The first two
        require the product rule, and for the third,
        <m>\deriv{}{t}\left(-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}t^{3/2}\right)
           =-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{2}\,t^{1/2}
           =-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{2}\,t^{1/2}=-\sqrt{2}\,t^{1/2}</m>. Hence

      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{v}(t) \amp = \frac{d\mathbf{r}(t)}{dt}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \left\langle \cos t-t\sin t,\ \sin t+t\cos t,\
                                -\sqrt{2}\,t^{1/2}\right\rangle.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Its length simplifies neatly: the cross terms
        <m>\pm 2t\sin t\cos t</m> cancel, the squared sines and cosines
        combine, and the last component contributes
        <m>(-\sqrt{2}\,t^{1/2})^2=2t</m>:

      <md>
        <mrow>
          \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert \amp
            = \sqrt{(\cos t-t\sin t)^2+(\sin t+t\cos t)^2+2t}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \sqrt{\left(\cos^2 t+\sin^2 t\right)
                     + t^2\left(\sin^2 t+\cos^2 t\right) + 2t}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \sqrt{t^2+2t+1}=\sqrt{(t+1)^2}=t+1,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        where the last equality uses <m>t+1&gt;0</m>. So the paraglider's
        speed grows linearly: it starts at <m>1</m> and reaches <m>10</m> at
        touchdown.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> The distance travelled is the arc length
        <xref ref="eq-arc-length-3d"/> of the
        path, that is, speed integrated over the <m>9</m> units of flight
        time:

      <md>
        <mrow>
          S \amp = \int_0^{9}\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert\,dt
               = \int_0^{9}(t+1)\,dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \left[\frac{t^2}{2}+t\right]_0^{9}
               = \frac{81}{2}+9=49.5.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The descending spiral, together with its endpoints
        <m>\left\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\right\rangle</m> and
        <m>\left\langle 9\cos 9,\,9\sin 9,\,0\right\rangle</m>, is animated
        in <xref ref="fig-paraglider-video"/> and shown in
        <xref ref="fig-paraglider-tikz"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-paraglider-video">
        <caption>
          The paraglider spirals down from
          <m>\left\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\right\rangle</m> to
          <m>\left\langle 9\cos 9,\,9\sin 9,\,0\right\rangle</m>. Because
          the speed is
          <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert=t+1</m>, the total
          distance travelled is <m>S=49.5</m>.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-paraglider" width="85%"
               source="videos/paraglider_arclength_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-paraglider-tikz">
        <caption>
          The paraglider's path from
          <m>\left\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\right\rangle</m> down to
          <m>\left\langle 9\cos 9,\,9\sin 9,\,0\right\rangle</m>. The dashed
          gray curve is the path's shadow on the ground <m>z=0</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-paraglider-tikz" width="75%">
          <shortdescription>
            The paraglider's descending spiral path in three dimensions,
            from its starting point down to the ground.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
              minus 10 to 10 and z from 0 to 27. A thick blue spiral starts
              at a red point labelled start, at the position vector with
              components 0, 0, and 9 root 8, about 25.5 units above the
              origin. The spiral winds outward and downward, its radius
              growing as it descends, making roughly one and a half turns
              before landing at a red point labelled ground, at the position
              vector with components 9 cosine 9, 9 sine 9, and 0. A dashed
              gray spiral on the floor of the box shows the path's shadow on
              the ground.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
                width=12cm, view={115}{20},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-10, xmax=10, ymin=-10, ymax=10, zmin=0, zmax=27,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20},
              ]
              % shadow of the path on the ground z=0
              \addplot3[gray!50, dashed, thin, domain=0:9, samples=200, samples y=0]
                  ({x*cos(deg(x))}, {x*sin(deg(x))}, {0});
              % the paraglider's path
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:9, samples=200, samples y=0]
                  ({x*cos(deg(x))}, {x*sin(deg(x))}, {9*sqrt(8)-sqrt(8)/3*x^1.5});
              % start and end points
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates
                  {(0,0,25.4558) (-8.2003,3.7091,0)};
              \node[above right] at (axis cs:0,0,25.4558)
                  {start \(\left\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\right\rangle\)};
              \node[below] at (axis cs:-8.2003,3.7091,0)
                  {ground \(\left\langle 9\cos 9,\ 9\sin 9,\ 0\right\rangle\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- ACTIVITY: where a plane slices a cone. Three tasks: parametrize,   -->
  <!-- arc length between P and Q, arc-length function from the vertex.   -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-cone-plane-activity">
    <title>Activity: Where a Plane Slices a Cone</title>

  <activity xml:id="activity-cone-plane-arc-length">
    <title>Where a Plane Slices a Cone</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>S_1</m> be the upper half of a circular cone and let
        <m>S_2</m> be a slanted plane:

      <md>
        S_1 : \ z^2 = x^2 + y^2, \quad z \ge 0,
        \qquad\qquad S_2 : \ z = x + 1 .
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>C = S_1 \cap S_2</m> be their curve of intersection, shown in
        <xref ref="fig-cone-plane"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cone-plane">
        <caption>
          The cone <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m> with <m>z \ge 0</m>, the plane
          <m>z = x+1</m>, and the curve <m>C</m> where they meet. The darker
          portion is the arc from <m>P(0,-1,1)</m> to <m>Q(0,1,1)</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-cone-plane" width="78%">
          <shortdescription>
            An upward-opening cone cut by a slanted plane, with the parabola
            along which they meet drawn on the surfaces.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional plot showing the upper nappe of a circular
              cone opening upward from the origin, a slanted plane cutting
              through it, and the parabola along which the two surfaces meet.
              The vertex of the parabola is marked V at its lowest point, and
              the points P and Q, where the parabola crosses the plane x
              equals zero, are marked on either side of the vertex.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
                width=12cm, view={125}{18},
                x dir=reverse, y dir=reverse,
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-3.2, xmax=3.2, ymin=-3.2, ymax=3.2, zmin=0, zmax=3.4,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20},
              ]
              % the upper nappe of the cone, as (u cos v, u sin v, u)
              \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.35, draw=gray!45,
                        colormap={conegray}{color=(gray!20) color=(gray!55)},
                        domain=0:3.2, y domain=0:2*pi,
                        samples=18, samples y=48]
                  ({x*cos(deg(y))}, {x*sin(deg(y))}, {x});
              % the plane z = x+1
              \draw[blue!55, fill=blue!25, fill opacity=0.35]
                  (axis cs:-1,-3.2,0) -- (axis cs:-1,3.2,0)
                  -- (axis cs:2.4,3.2,3.4) -- (axis cs:2.4,-3.2,3.4) -- cycle;
              % the curve of intersection C
              \addplot3[red, very thick, domain=-2.3:2.3,
                        samples=200, samples y=0]
                  ({(x^2-1)/2}, {x}, {(x^2+1)/2});
              % the arc of C from P to Q
              \addplot3[red!55!black, ultra thick, domain=-1:1,
                        samples=80, samples y=0]
                  ({(x^2-1)/2}, {x}, {(x^2+1)/2});
              % the vertex and the two endpoints
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, black, mark size=1.4pt]
                  coordinates {(-0.5,0,0.5) (0,-1,1) (0,1,1)};
              \node[below left] at (axis cs:-0.5,0,0.5) {\(V\)};
              \node[right] at (axis cs:0,-1,1) {\(P\)};
              \node[left]  at (axis cs:0,1,1)  {\(Q\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ............................................................... -->
    <task xml:id="task-cone-plane-parametrize">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that every point of <m>C</m> satisfies <m>y^2 = 2x + 1</m>, and
          use this to find a vector parametrization
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle x(t),\,y(t),\,z(t)\right\rangle</m>
          of <m>C</m>. Then verify directly that your <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>
          lies on <em>both</em> surfaces for every <m>t</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Suggestion.</em> Use <m>y</m> itself as the parameter, and say
          why that is smarter than using <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          A point lies on <m>C</m> exactly when it satisfies both equations at
          once. Substituting <m>z = x+1</m> from the plane into the equation of
          the cone eliminates <m>z</m>:

        <md>
          <mrow>
            (x+1)^2 \amp = x^2 + y^2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \Longrightarrow\quad x^2 + 2x + 1 \amp = x^2 + y^2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \Longrightarrow\quad \amp \boxed{\,y^2 = 2x+1\,} .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          So the shadow of <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the parabola
          <m>y^2 = 2x+1</m>, that is, <m>x = \frac{y^2-1}{2}</m>
          (<xref ref="fig-cone-plane-shadow"/>). Solving for <m>x</m> rather
          than for <m>y</m> is the key move: <m>x</m> is a
          <em>single-valued</em> function of <m>y</m>, so taking <m>y = t</m>
          as the parameter captures the whole curve at once, whereas
          parametrizing by <m>x</m> would force the two branches
          <m>y = \pm\sqrt{2x+1}</m> and split the problem in half. Since
          <m>z = x+1</m>,

        <men xml:id="eq-cone-plane-param">
          \mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle \frac{t^2-1}{2},\ \ t,\ \
          \frac{t^2+1}{2} \right\rangle , \qquad t \in \R .
        </men>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-cone-plane-shadow">
          <caption>
            The shadow of <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the parabola
            <m>y^2 = 2x+1</m>, with vertex <m>V</m>. The darker arc is the
            projection of the piece of <m>C</m> that runs from <m>P</m> to
            <m>Q</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-cone-plane-shadow" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
              A parabola opening to the right on a coordinate grid, with its
              vertex and the arc between the points (0,-1) and (0,1) marked.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                The shadow of the curve C in the xy-plane: a parabola opening
                to the right with vertex V at the point minus one half, zero.
                The arc between the points (0,-1) and (0,1), the shadow of the
                piece of C from P to Q, is drawn more thickly.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="cone-plane-shadow-parabola">
              <diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="5">
                <definition>p(t) = ((t^2-1)/2, t)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-2.4,2.4,2.4)">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                  <parametric-curve at="curve" function="p"
                                    domain="(-2.2,2.2)"
                                    stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
                  <parametric-curve at="arc" function="p"
                                    domain="(-1,1)"
                                    stroke="darkred" thickness="4"/>
                  <point p="(-0.5,0)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>V</m>
                  </point>
                  <point p="(0,1)" alignment="ne">
                    <m>(0,1)</m>
                  </point>
                  <point p="(0,-1)" alignment="se">
                    <m>(0,-1)</m>
                  </point>
                  <label anchor="(1.15,-1.75)" alignment="ne">
                    <m>y^2 = 2x+1</m>
                  </label>
                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="The shadow of the curve C in the xy-plane,
                                    a parabola opening to the right with
                                    vertex at the point (-1/2, 0)">
                    <annotation ref="curve"
                                text="The parabola y squared equals 2x plus 1"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="arc"
                                text="The arc of the parabola between the
                                      points (0,-1) and (0,1), the shadow of
                                      the piece of C from P to Q"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <em>Check on <m>S_1</m>:</em>

        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^2 + y^2 \amp = \frac{t^4 - 2t^2 + 1}{4} + t^2
                          = \frac{t^4 + 2t^2 + 1}{4}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \left( \frac{t^2+1}{2} \right)^{2} = z^2 ,
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          and <m>z = \frac{t^2+1}{2} \gt 0</m> for all <m>t</m>, so the entire
          curve lies on the upper nappe <m>z \ge 0</m>.
          <em>Check on <m>S_2</m>:</em>
          <m>z - x = \frac{t^2+1}{2} - \frac{t^2-1}{2} = 1</m>, so
          <m>z = x+1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ............................................................... -->
    <task xml:id="task-cone-plane-arc-length">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The curve <m>C</m> crosses the plane <m>x = 0</m> at the two points
          <m>P(0,-1,1)</m> and <m>Q(0,1,1)</m>. Set up the arc-length integral
          <xref ref="eq-arc-length-3d"/>
          for the piece of <m>C</m> joining <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m> and evaluate
          it <em>exactly</em>. Then give a decimal approximation to four
          places.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              S \amp = \sqrt{3} + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
                       \ln\!\left( \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \sqrt{3}
                     + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\sqrt{2}\right)
                   \approx 2.5425 .
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>

      <solution>
        <p>
          First locate the endpoints: <m>x = 0</m> means
          <m>\frac{t^2-1}{2} = 0</m>, so <m>t = \pm 1</m>, giving
          <m>\mathbf{r}(-1) = (0,-1,1) = P</m> and
          <m>\mathbf{r}(1) = (0,1,1) = Q</m>. Differentiating the
          parametrization <xref ref="eq-cone-plane-param"/> componentwise
          gives the velocity vector

        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{v}(t) \amp = \left\langle t,\, 1,\, t \right\rangle ,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert \amp
              = \sqrt{t^2 + 1 + t^2} = \sqrt{1 + 2t^2} .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Hence, using the evenness of the integrand,

        <md>
          <mrow>
            S \amp = \int_{-1}^{1}
                       \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert \, dt
                   = \int_{-1}^{1} \sqrt{1+2t^2} \, dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = 2\int_{0}^{1} \sqrt{1+2t^2} \, dt .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The integrand is not quite of the form <m>\sqrt{a^2+u^2}</m>, so
          first substitute <m>u = \sqrt{2}\,t</m>, <m>du = \sqrt{2}\,dt</m>,
          which sends <m>t : 0 \to 1</m> to <m>u : 0 \to \sqrt{2}</m>:

        <md>
          <mrow>
            S \amp = \frac{2}{\sqrt{2}}
                     \int_{0}^{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{1+u^2} \, du
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sqrt{2} \int_{0}^{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{1+u^2} \, du .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now evaluate <m>I = \int \sqrt{1+u^2}\,du</m>. Integrating by parts
          against <m>1</m> reproduces the original integral, which lets us
          solve for it: with <m>u^2 = (1+u^2) - 1</m> in the numerator,

        <md>
          <mrow>
            I \amp = u\sqrt{1+u^2}
                     - \int \frac{u^2}{\sqrt{1+u^2}} \, du
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = u\sqrt{1+u^2}
                   - \int \frac{(1+u^2) - 1}{\sqrt{1+u^2}} \, du
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = u\sqrt{1+u^2} - I
                   + \int \frac{du}{\sqrt{1+u^2}} .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last integral is <m>\sinh^{-1} u</m>, so
          <m>2I = u\sqrt{1+u^2} + \sinh^{-1} u</m>, that is,<fn>
          The inverse hyperbolic sine is a logarithm in disguise. Setting
          <m>w = \sinh y = \frac{e^{y} - e^{-y}}{2}</m> and multiplying
          through by <m>2e^{y}</m> gives
          <m>\left(e^{y}\right)^2 - 2we^{y} - 1 = 0</m>, a quadratic in
          <m>e^{y}</m>. The quadratic formula gives
          <m>e^{y} = w \pm \sqrt{w^2+1}</m>, and only the <m>+</m> sign keeps
          <m>e^{y}</m> positive, so
          <m>\sinh^{-1} w = y = \ln\!\left(w + \sqrt{1+w^2}\right)</m>.
          </fn>

        <md>
          <mrow xml:id="eq-sqrt-antiderivative" number="yes">
            \int \sqrt{1+u^2} \, du \amp = \frac{u}{2}\sqrt{1+u^2}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \quad + \frac{1}{2}
                   \ln\!\left( u + \sqrt{1+u^2} \right) + C .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The substitution <m>u = \sinh w</m> gives the same antiderivative:
          it turns the integral into <m>\int \cosh^2 w \, dw</m>. The
          trigonometric substitution <m>u = \tan\theta</m> works too, but it
          leads to <m>\int \sec^3\theta \, d\theta</m>, which needs the same
          solve-for-<m>I</m> trick anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally apply the limits. At <m>u = \sqrt{2}</m> we have
          <m>\sqrt{1+u^2} = \sqrt{3}</m>, and everything vanishes at
          <m>u = 0</m>:

        <md>
          <mrow>
            S \amp = \sqrt{2}\left[ \frac{u}{2}\sqrt{1+u^2}
                     + \frac{1}{2}\ln\!\left( u + \sqrt{1+u^2} \right)
                     \right]_{0}^{\sqrt{2}}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sqrt{2}\left[ \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\sqrt{3}
                   + \frac{1}{2}\ln\!\left( \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3} \right)
                   \right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sqrt{3} + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
                   \ln\!\left( \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3} \right)
                 \approx 2.5425 .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Sanity check: the straight-line distance from <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m> is
          <m>2</m>, and <m>2.5425 \gt 2</m>, as an arc must be.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ............................................................... -->
    <task xml:id="task-cone-plane-arc-length-function">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The point <m>V\left(-\frac{1}{2},\, 0,\, \frac{1}{2}\right)</m> is
          the vertex (the lowest point) of <m>C</m>. Find the arc-length
          function <m>s(t)</m> measured from <m>V</m>, and use it to compute
          the exact length of <m>C</m> from <m>V</m> to the point
          <m>\left(\frac{3}{2},\, 2,\, \frac{5}{2}\right)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            s(2) = 3 + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\ln\!\left( 1 + \sqrt{2} \right)
                 \approx 3.6232 .
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The vertex <m>V</m> is <m>\mathbf{r}(0)</m> in
          <xref ref="eq-cone-plane-param"/>, so measuring arc length
          from <m>V</m> means starting the integral at <m>0</m> and
          applying the antiderivative <xref ref="eq-sqrt-antiderivative"/>:

        <md>
          <mrow>
            s(t) \amp = \int_{0}^{t}
                          \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(\tau)\right\rVert \, d\tau
                      = \int_{0}^{t} \sqrt{1 + 2\tau^2} \, d\tau
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{t}{2}\sqrt{1+2t^2}
                   + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}
                     \ln\!\left( \sqrt{2}\,t + \sqrt{1+2t^2} \right) ,
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          which is also written

        <md>
          s(t) = \frac{t}{2}\sqrt{1+2t^2}
                 + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\sqrt{2}\,t\right) .
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          One can verify
          <m>s'(t) = \sqrt{1+2t^2}
             = \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert</m>, as it must be. The
          point <m>\left(\frac{3}{2}, 2, \frac{5}{2}\right)</m> corresponds to
          <m>t = 2</m>, since <m>y = t = 2</m> and then
          <m>\frac{t^2-1}{2} = \frac{3}{2}</m> and
          <m>\frac{t^2+1}{2} = \frac{5}{2}</m>. Therefore

        <md>
          <mrow>
            s(2) \amp = \frac{2}{2}\sqrt{1+8}
                        + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}
                          \ln\!\left( 2\sqrt{2} + 3 \right)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = 3 + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}
                       \ln\!\left( 3 + 2\sqrt{2} \right) .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Because <m>3 + 2\sqrt{2} = \left( 1 + \sqrt{2} \right)^{2}</m>, the
          logarithm halves:

        <md>
          s(2) = 3 + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\ln\!\left( 1 + \sqrt{2} \right)
               \approx 3.6232 .
        </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <remark xml:id="remark-cone-plane-instructor-notes">
    <title>Instructor notes<mdash/>what to watch for</title>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Losing half the curve.</term> Students who write
          <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and then square are fine, but those who
          parametrize by <m>x</m> get <m>y = \pm\sqrt{2x+1}</m> and often keep
          only the <m>+</m> branch<mdash/>losing the arc from <m>P</m> to
          <m>V</m>. The check <m>z = \frac{t^2+1}{2} \gt 0</m> in the first
          task is worth insisting on.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Forgetting to verify.</term> The first task asks for a
          substitution <em>and</em> a check; a parametrization that satisfies
          only one of the two equations is a common error.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Wrong endpoints.</term> In the arc-length task some students
          integrate over <m>0 \le t \le 1</m> and forget the symmetric half,
          getting exactly half the correct length.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>
  </subsection>

</section>
